{"name":"Ballroom Blue","hex":"#a6b3c9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ballroom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large room used for dancing and banquets.","A type of elegant dance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To take part in ballroom dancing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ballroom"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ballroom","blocks":[{"text":"A ballroom or ballhall is a large room for holding large formal parties called balls. Traditionally, most balls were held in private residences; many mansions and palaces, especially historic mansions and palaces, contain one or more ballrooms. In other large houses, a large room such as the main drawing room, long gallery, or hall may double as a ballroom, but a good ballroom should have the right type of flooring, such as hardwood flooring or stone flooring .. For most styles of modern dance, a wooden sprung floor offers the best surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Catherine_Palace_ballroom.jpg/330px-Catherine_Palace_ballroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballroom","wikidata":"Q805353"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Oranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_%27s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg/500px-Oranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_%27s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Oranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_%27s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg/1280px-Oranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_%27s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Oranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_%27s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg/500px-Oranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_%27s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Oranjezaal in palace Huis ten Bosch in The Hague, The Netherlands ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOranjezaal_na_de_restauratie-_overzicht_noordoosthoek%2C_met_de_hele_oostwand_-_'s-Gravenhage_-_20416714_-_RCE.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thum